WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.276 --> 00:00:07.000
Hi everybody,
I'm Sze-Chuan Suen.

00:00:07.001 --> 00:00:08.510
I'm a faculty member
at the Industrial and

00:00:08.511 --> 00:00:10.490
Systems Engineering at USC.

00:00:10.491 --> 00:00:13.530
And today I'm very pleased to
be talking to you about some of

00:00:13.531 --> 00:00:17.150
the projects that I've done on
tuberculosis or TB in India.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:21.690
So TB now kills more
people than HIV worldwide.

00:00:21.691 --> 00:00:23.730
Which is surprising
to a lot of people.

00:00:23.731 --> 00:00:26.110
And most of my work
in focused on India.

00:00:26.111 --> 00:00:28.080
In the contexts of
Indian Tuberculosis because

00:00:28.081 --> 00:00:31.140
about a quarter of the worlds
cases reside here.

00:00:31.141 --> 00:00:35.020
And besides many of the drug
sensitive or DS cases in TB.

00:00:35.021 --> 00:00:39.045
One of the problems facing India
is a growing number of drug

00:00:39.046 --> 00:00:44.056
resistant tuberculosis cases of
which about 3% of all cases are.

00:00:44.057 --> 00:00:47.533
So drug resistant TB will
happen when patients undergo

00:00:47.534 --> 00:00:51.541
antiboditic therapy but they
don't finish their regiments or

00:00:51.542 --> 00:00:54.652
can also occur because
of direct transmission.

00:00:54.653 --> 00:00:57.991
So I'll talk about both of
those pathways today and

00:00:57.992 --> 00:01:02.109
the problem that drug resistant
or DRTB presents is really that

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:05.916
it requires treatment with
second line antibiotics which

00:01:05.917 --> 00:01:09.503
can be more costly and can be
more toxic to the patient.

00:01:09.504 --> 00:01:12.263
And can also be very
difficult to diagnose because

00:01:12.264 --> 00:01:15.548
these patients will present with
the same symptoms as a drug

00:01:15.549 --> 00:01:16.872
sensitive TB patient.

00:01:16.873 --> 00:01:19.402
So I'm gonna talk about some of
the work that I have done in

00:01:19.403 --> 00:01:19.990
these area.

00:01:19.991 --> 00:01:21.871
Obviously there is a lot
of projects here, so

00:01:21.872 --> 00:01:23.463
I'm not gonna talk
about all of them.

00:01:23.464 --> 00:01:25.040
But I welcome questions
on any of them.

00:01:25.041 --> 00:01:28.267
I won't talk about this Gates
Foundation Model Comparison that

00:01:28.268 --> 00:01:31.311
I've done in the past, and Ryan
here will be talking a little

00:01:31.312 --> 00:01:34.179
bit more about the TB outreach
resource allocation model

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:35.840
towards the end of this talk.

00:01:35.841 --> 00:01:38.810
And Cara will introduce some of
the topics that we have been

00:01:38.811 --> 00:01:41.810
talking about for collaboration
on predicting treatment default.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:45.900
But to move on to some of
the overview of what I've done

00:01:45.901 --> 00:01:46.940
before.

00:01:46.941 --> 00:01:49.460
One of the models that
I originally built was

00:01:49.461 --> 00:01:52.340
an individual level
simulation on predicting drug

00:01:52.341 --> 00:01:54.850
resistance TB
problems prediction.

00:01:54.851 --> 00:01:58.286
So in this model, we followed
individuals from birth until

00:01:58.287 --> 00:02:01.103
death moving through
different health states,

00:02:01.104 --> 00:02:04.263
whether a latent drug sensitive
or drug resistant TB,

00:02:04.264 --> 00:02:06.617
active disease,
as well as treatment.

00:02:06.618 --> 00:02:09.568
And we wanted to make this
model as detailed as possible.

00:02:09.569 --> 00:02:12.907
And since we were modelling
Indian Tuberculosis, we included

00:02:12.908 --> 00:02:15.616
many of the treatment
options that are available in

00:02:15.617 --> 00:02:18.325
India such as private treatment,
as well as the one

00:02:18.326 --> 00:02:21.853
that's offered by the federal
government, public treatment for

00:02:21.854 --> 00:02:24.480
first line,
as well as second line therapy.

00:02:24.481 --> 00:02:26.900
So this is a pretty busy slide
just to guide you through it.

00:02:26.901 --> 00:02:27.688
We looked at for

00:02:27.689 --> 00:02:30.302
instance patient undergoing
first line therapy.

00:02:30.303 --> 00:02:33.675
We looked into the literature
for the RNTCP or

00:02:33.676 --> 00:02:37.862
the public funding program
that treats tuberculosis.

00:02:37.863 --> 00:02:40.882
It gives us six months of the
treatment for each patient and

00:02:40.883 --> 00:02:43.593
there were monthly risks of
death and default that we

00:02:43.594 --> 00:02:46.798
incorporated into the simulation
so that we would accurately

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:50.200
model how patients behaved
on these treatment programs.

00:02:50.201 --> 00:02:54.151
As well as things like drug
resistant testing which patients

00:02:54.152 --> 00:02:57.563
undergo in first line
therapy at around month four.

00:02:57.564 --> 00:03:00.403
And they could exit off of
treatment, either successfully

00:03:00.404 --> 00:03:03.470
treated or with active or latent
tuberculosis if they default or

00:03:03.471 --> 00:03:04.685
failed from treatment.

00:03:04.686 --> 00:03:07.309
And so
we wanted to be realistic and

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:10.754
capture these patterns
as much as possible.

00:03:10.755 --> 00:03:13.019
In addition, we wanted to
include demographic and

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:14.178
behavioral factors and so

00:03:14.179 --> 00:03:16.983
a lot of these were informed how
tuberculosis is transmitted.

00:03:16.984 --> 00:03:19.503
So for instance, transmission
rates varied by age and

00:03:19.504 --> 00:03:23.138
reflected household compositions
within the simulation.

00:03:23.139 --> 00:03:27.190
Transition rates depended on
natural history, and age, and

00:03:27.191 --> 00:03:30.600
competing mortality risks also
dependent on age and sex.

00:03:30.601 --> 00:03:33.694
And in the simulation we
had treatment uptake and

00:03:33.695 --> 00:03:36.864
adherence and success that
varies by age and sex,

00:03:36.865 --> 00:03:40.653
as well as by the time and what
time it was in the simulation.

00:03:40.654 --> 00:03:43.333
So for instance,
we had the model Burn In so

00:03:43.334 --> 00:03:45.946
that our demographics
would stabilize.

00:03:45.947 --> 00:03:49.492
So our patient population that
we simulated within the model

00:03:49.493 --> 00:03:52.621
would reflect that age
stratifications that we saw in

00:03:52.622 --> 00:03:53.316
India, and

00:03:53.317 --> 00:03:56.601
that was very important to
get the population correct.

00:03:56.602 --> 00:03:59.609
And we also had population
growth within the simulation

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:02.809
because we know the demographics
within India are changing

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:03.449
over time.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:05.549
And so these are match
to both historical and

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:07.709
projections of Indian
population growth for

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:09.770
the United Nations
Population Division.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:14.339
Over time we had the federal
treatment program ramp up and so

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:17.134
in the simulation we had
more and more patients,

00:04:17.135 --> 00:04:20.493
have access to tuberculosis
treatment over this period.

00:04:20.494 --> 00:04:24.165
And over that time, we can see
that the simulation estimates

00:04:24.166 --> 00:04:27.189
here depicted in red are very
similar to those that

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:30.717
were given by the WHO from this
period for both problems and

00:04:30.718 --> 00:04:32.520
incidents of tuberculosis.

00:04:32.521 --> 00:04:35.780
So we can be more confident that
the simulation is performing

00:04:35.781 --> 00:04:36.700
in an accurate way.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.980
We also had second line therapy
ramp up over the appropriate

00:04:40.981 --> 00:04:42.580
period in the simulation.

00:04:42.581 --> 00:04:45.305
So which now we can do
projections about the future to

00:04:45.306 --> 00:04:47.440
inform us about how
the TB prevalence and

00:04:47.441 --> 00:04:49.830
epidemic are going to
behave in the future.

00:04:49.831 --> 00:04:50.680
And over this period,

00:04:50.681 --> 00:04:53.500
we see circular trends
such as population aging

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:55.920
which we would expect to
see over this period.

00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:59.810
So now we have this really neat,
cool simulation.

00:04:59.811 --> 00:05:01.270
What can we do with it?

00:05:01.271 --> 00:05:04.210
So one of the interesting things
that we could do was to look at

00:05:04.211 --> 00:05:06.400
how people acquire
drug resistant TB.

00:05:06.401 --> 00:05:09.475
And this is a problem that you
can't actually go and ask people

00:05:09.476 --> 00:05:12.683
about because people don't know
where they got tuberculosis.

00:05:12.684 --> 00:05:15.293
And so, for these cases of
drug resistant TB which

00:05:15.294 --> 00:05:17.904
the Government of India
is very keen on reducing,

00:05:17.905 --> 00:05:21.031
we could split them apart in
transmission generated case.

00:05:21.032 --> 00:05:25.101
So people got coughed on and
they acquired drug resistant TB,

00:05:25.102 --> 00:05:28.389
versus those that were
rising because of failed or

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:30.753
incomplete treatment regiments.

00:05:30.754 --> 00:05:33.536
And we see that over time, this
simulation will project that

00:05:33.537 --> 00:05:36.213
a larger and larger proportion
of people are getting cases

00:05:36.214 --> 00:05:38.300
directly through transmission.

00:05:38.301 --> 00:05:39.470
And this has implications for

00:05:39.471 --> 00:05:42.030
how we can treat drug-resistant
TB and how we can

00:05:42.031 --> 00:05:44.730
interrupt the process of
people getting these diseases.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.510
The second topic I'm going
to touch a little bit on

00:05:49.511 --> 00:05:52.445
is looking at policy
prioritization and looking at

00:05:52.446 --> 00:05:56.330
cost-effectiveness analysis
to inform policy decisions.

00:05:56.331 --> 00:06:00.000
So for instance, the government
of India is planning to acquire

00:06:00.001 --> 00:06:03.570
expensive new GeneXpert
diagnosis systems,

00:06:03.571 --> 00:06:06.330
which allows for
more accurate TB diagnosis, and

00:06:06.331 --> 00:06:09.030
this also very quickly
able to distinguish

00:06:09.031 --> 00:06:12.060
between drug sensitive and
drug resistant tuberculosis.

00:06:12.061 --> 00:06:13.920
Now these are very
expensive systems.

00:06:13.921 --> 00:06:15.330
So we wanted to inform
the government about

00:06:15.331 --> 00:06:17.110
how they should use them.

00:06:17.111 --> 00:06:20.740
So one of the ways that we could
use them is to just use them for

00:06:20.741 --> 00:06:23.320
drug resistant testing at
month number four in first

00:06:23.321 --> 00:06:24.220
line therapy.

00:06:24.221 --> 00:06:27.600
But we can also introduce these
systems for initial diagnosis.

00:06:27.601 --> 00:06:29.070
So we wanted to look
at the costs and

00:06:29.071 --> 00:06:31.258
the health impacts of doing
both of these polices.

00:06:31.259 --> 00:06:34.780
And we compare them against
institutional reforms

00:06:34.781 --> 00:06:37.970
like public private mixes or PPM
programs which the government of

00:06:37.971 --> 00:06:40.050
India is also considering doing.

00:06:40.051 --> 00:06:42.800
Where private clinic patients
are referred to public clinics,

00:06:42.801 --> 00:06:44.890
because public clinics generally
have more oversight and

00:06:44.891 --> 00:06:47.340
more informed doctors that
are looking specifically for

00:06:47.341 --> 00:06:48.930
TB patients.

00:06:48.931 --> 00:06:51.540
We also looked at combination
strategies of these different

00:06:51.541 --> 00:06:52.580
policies.

00:06:52.581 --> 00:06:56.100
And by informing the simulation,
the individual level simulation

00:06:56.101 --> 00:06:59.410
with information about costs
as well as health outcomes,

00:06:59.411 --> 00:07:01.910
we can form the cost
effective frontier for

00:07:01.911 --> 00:07:03.840
all of the policies
that we considered.

00:07:03.841 --> 00:07:07.660
Being able then to identify
the tradeoffs between health and

00:07:07.661 --> 00:07:11.160
costs and tell the government
where there were inefficient

00:07:11.161 --> 00:07:13.960
polices that were not lying
on that affected frontier.

00:07:13.961 --> 00:07:17.350
And so this sort of analysis
can inform public policy

00:07:17.351 --> 00:07:18.270
in terms of healthcare.

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:23.240
The last problem that I
want to touch on is about

00:07:23.241 --> 00:07:26.006
when to test for
drug resistant tuberculosis and

00:07:26.007 --> 00:07:28.110
this was solved
using a POMDP model.

00:07:28.111 --> 00:07:33.655
So there are two tests that is
used for first line therapy.

00:07:33.656 --> 00:07:36.239
Sputum Smear Tests or
SS Tests are very cheap but

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:38.053
they're also very inaccurate.

00:07:38.054 --> 00:07:41.113
They have very low sensitivity
so they're very noisy, and

00:07:41.114 --> 00:07:43.490
they also cannot detect
the disease strain.

00:07:43.491 --> 00:07:45.940
So these types of tests which
are currently used give us

00:07:45.941 --> 00:07:49.710
a very noisy estimate of whether
our patient has TB or not.

00:07:49.711 --> 00:07:52.400
Drug Sensitivity Testing or
DST, which we'll

00:07:52.401 --> 00:07:56.080
consider in the context of these
expensive GeneXpert systems,

00:07:56.081 --> 00:07:57.850
are very expensive as
I've told you before, but

00:07:57.851 --> 00:08:00.280
they're very accurate and they
can detect the disease strain.

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:04.150
So the question we asked here
was how we could use this noisy

00:08:04.151 --> 00:08:07.300
test to identify which
patients need DST, and

00:08:07.301 --> 00:08:08.740
when they should undergo DST?

00:08:08.741 --> 00:08:10.777
So we want to have
high risk patients and

00:08:10.778 --> 00:08:13.432
use that expensive but
very accurate test on them so

00:08:13.433 --> 00:08:16.661
we can triage them into the
appropriate second line therapy.

00:08:16.662 --> 00:08:17.519
Right now DST,

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:20.621
drug sensitive testing is
being done at month four, but

00:08:20.622 --> 00:08:24.119
with this new technology It's
unclear whether or not we should

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:27.501
do it earlier or later in that
first line therapy treatment.

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:32.504
An so we wanted to find patients
specific DST times that would

00:08:32.505 --> 00:08:35.680
provent unnecessary testing
which is high financial costs.

00:08:35.681 --> 00:08:38.219
But we did don't want to do
it because it will accelerate

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:40.003
triage into appropriate
treatment for

00:08:40.004 --> 00:08:42.773
those patients that did have
drug resistant tuberculosis.

00:08:42.774 --> 00:08:45.681
Now recall only about 3%
of the TB cases in India,

00:08:45.682 --> 00:08:47.180
have drug resistant TB.

00:08:47.181 --> 00:08:48.670
So it's a pretty low population.

00:08:48.671 --> 00:08:51.610
So we wanna focus on
these high risk patients

00:08:51.611 --> 00:08:54.120
that we would be able to
identify using this test.

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:57.888
So just to give you
an overview of our palm DP,

00:08:57.889 --> 00:09:01.616
at every month there would some
belief state that the physician

00:09:01.617 --> 00:09:03.656
had about the patient,
whether or

00:09:03.657 --> 00:09:07.244
not they were successfully
treated, whether they still had

00:09:07.245 --> 00:09:09.871
drug sensitive TB or
had drug resistant TB.

00:09:09.872 --> 00:09:13.266
They are then able to do three
actions, either DST that patient

00:09:13.267 --> 00:09:16.021
using that expensive test
that's very accurate,

00:09:16.022 --> 00:09:19.033
or have the patient continue
on treatment either with or

00:09:19.034 --> 00:09:21.690
without a noisy test from
the spetum smear test.

00:09:21.691 --> 00:09:24.550
The patient would undergo
treatment and if the physician

00:09:24.551 --> 00:09:27.760
wanted to test the patient
receive a noisy positive or

00:09:27.761 --> 00:09:31.620
negative test result from
the patient and then be able to

00:09:31.621 --> 00:09:33.970
update their belief about
the patient's health state.

00:09:33.971 --> 00:09:36.919
So this will continue for
the six months of TB treatment.

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:41.030
And we ran our palm DP in
order to find the result, and

00:09:41.031 --> 00:09:44.720
we found that in fact it was
optimal to DST the patient had

00:09:44.721 --> 00:09:45.980
initial diagnosis.

00:09:45.981 --> 00:09:49.060
This is somewhat surprising
because there's very few number

00:09:49.061 --> 00:09:51.650
of TB cases that have
drug resistant TB, and so

00:09:51.651 --> 00:09:53.840
we would be unnecessarily
testing a lot of people,

00:09:53.841 --> 00:09:55.680
which has high financial costs.

00:09:55.681 --> 00:09:57.950
Now this is in fact driven
by TB transmission,

00:09:57.951 --> 00:10:00.770
because those downstream costs
of further transmission,

00:10:00.771 --> 00:10:03.140
identifying those people,
bringing them into treatment and

00:10:03.141 --> 00:10:05.820
then treating them with second
line therapy was so high.

00:10:05.821 --> 00:10:09.130
It was worth it to do that
unnecessary testing upfront.

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:13.330
So those are some of the topics
that I've done research on, and

00:10:13.331 --> 00:10:17.370
Bryan will now talk about
TB outreach programs and

00:10:17.371 --> 00:10:18.690
resource allocation.

00:10:18.691 --> 00:10:19.330
&gt;&gt; Hi, everyone, so

00:10:19.331 --> 00:10:21.950
my name is Bryan Wilder and I'll
be picking up with some recent

00:10:21.951 --> 00:10:23.660
ongoing work with
tuberculosis in India.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:27.870
So Sze already covered
the basic motivation here.

00:10:27.871 --> 00:10:30.680
And the focus of this work is
how do we get more patients into

00:10:30.681 --> 00:10:32.420
treatment for
tuberculosis in the first place,

00:10:32.421 --> 00:10:34.410
because treatment is
provided for free, right?

00:10:34.411 --> 00:10:36.240
So this is one of
the major bottlenecks.

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:41.691
And a common strategy for this
is outreach campaigns, right.

00:10:41.692 --> 00:10:43.890
So, patients either don't know
tuberculosis symptoms, or

00:10:43.891 --> 00:10:45.315
they don't know
the treatment is available,

00:10:45.316 --> 00:10:47.960
and so the government
will conduct these

00:10:47.961 --> 00:10:51.020
advertising campaigns, right, to
raise awareness and get people

00:10:51.021 --> 00:10:53.660
into treatment when they think
that they may have TB symptoms.

00:10:53.661 --> 00:10:57.430
And so this is just one example
of such an advertisement, and

00:10:57.431 --> 00:10:58.260
there are many others, right.

00:10:58.261 --> 00:11:00.410
And there are many ways that we
could structure these campaigns.

00:11:00.411 --> 00:11:04.240
So, maybe the ad on the far left
here is aimed at parents of

00:11:04.241 --> 00:11:05.000
small children,

00:11:05.001 --> 00:11:07.740
the one in the middle is aimed
at sports fans, and so on.

00:11:07.741 --> 00:11:09.150
So there are many
different demographic

00:11:09.151 --> 00:11:11.350
groups that I can think
of advertising to.

00:11:11.351 --> 00:11:14.470
And I only have a limited amount
of money with which to conduct

00:11:14.471 --> 00:11:15.990
these campaigns, and so

00:11:15.991 --> 00:11:18.940
the question that we're
interested in in this work is

00:11:18.941 --> 00:11:21.560
given that I have this limited
budget how should I divide it up

00:11:21.561 --> 00:11:23.660
between different demographic
groups in the population?

00:11:23.661 --> 00:11:26.580
So who are the sorts of people
that I should focus the most

00:11:26.581 --> 00:11:29.688
resources on in order to have
the greatest impact on TB rates

00:11:29.689 --> 00:11:30.262
overall?

00:11:32.862 --> 00:11:36.599
So in this work we'll give
sort of a brief overview, so

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:40.341
we introduced an SIS style
multiagent model of disease

00:11:40.342 --> 00:11:41.480
spread.

00:11:41.481 --> 00:11:45.200
And then in this model we even
algorithm called DOMO to compute

00:11:45.201 --> 00:11:46.890
neuro optimal
resource allocations.

00:11:46.891 --> 00:11:49.720
So a division of a finite budget
between different demographic

00:11:49.721 --> 00:11:51.230
groups in the population.

00:11:51.231 --> 00:11:53.460
So we're able to both give
theoretical guarantees for

00:11:53.461 --> 00:11:57.240
this approach and then we also
conduct a data-driven evaluation

00:11:57.241 --> 00:12:00.080
and simulation of the policies
produced by our algorithm.

00:12:00.081 --> 00:12:04.020
And we find that by using
its recommendations

00:12:04.021 --> 00:12:06.670
compared to a extrapolation
of status quo policies

00:12:06.671 --> 00:12:09.870
we convert thousands of cases of
tuberculosis each year in India.

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:15.510
So as a brief overview, the
style of model that we have here

00:12:15.511 --> 00:12:17.950
in this work is
a compartmental model so

00:12:17.951 --> 00:12:20.900
we have different demographic
groups in this case

00:12:20.901 --> 00:12:22.347
a segmentation of
the population by age.

00:12:22.348 --> 00:12:25.169
And so there is a finite
population of agents having

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:28.252
these groups are born they age
they die they move between

00:12:28.253 --> 00:12:29.648
groups and so on.

00:12:29.649 --> 00:12:32.200
And so, and they also
transmit the disease so

00:12:32.201 --> 00:12:34.450
there are some agents
that are infected and

00:12:34.451 --> 00:12:35.990
agents in each group
have at some rate with

00:12:35.991 --> 00:12:38.160
which they come into contact
with agents in other groups.

00:12:38.161 --> 00:12:40.290
And if you come into contact
with an infected agent then you

00:12:40.291 --> 00:12:41.390
yourself become infected.

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:46.820
So then when we think
about optimal targeting of

00:12:46.821 --> 00:12:49.730
resources in this model there
are a number of challenges.

00:12:49.731 --> 00:12:52.510
So you might say well
why don't I just

00:12:52.511 --> 00:12:55.240
target the groups that have
the most infected agents, right?

00:12:55.241 --> 00:12:56.850
That's where I'll sort
of get the most bang for

00:12:56.851 --> 00:12:58.950
my buck in terms of
targeting advertisements.

00:12:58.951 --> 00:13:02.420
But this doesn't work on its
own because maybe some groups

00:13:02.421 --> 00:13:04.090
are more important for
between groups, right?

00:13:04.091 --> 00:13:07.030
So if there is a particular age
range that is in contact with

00:13:07.031 --> 00:13:10.040
lots of others, then even
if they don't have as many

00:13:10.041 --> 00:13:11.610
infected agents in
terms of sure number,

00:13:11.611 --> 00:13:13.240
maybe you just focus on
advertisements there

00:13:13.241 --> 00:13:14.679
to reduce prevalence
rates down the stream.

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:18.670
But that on its own also isn't
sufficient, right, because that

00:13:18.671 --> 00:13:21.530
would suggest, okay, maybe I
should look at contact patterns

00:13:21.531 --> 00:13:26.120
and just target advertisements
at the groups that have the most

00:13:26.121 --> 00:13:27.290
sort of contact with others.

00:13:27.291 --> 00:13:29.440
But that also isn't optimal
because you have to think

00:13:29.441 --> 00:13:31.910
about the demographics of
the population and how

00:13:31.911 --> 00:13:34.610
things will evolve over time as
people age and move and so on.

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:39.390
Then complicating all of this
right, these difficulties and

00:13:39.391 --> 00:13:41.390
what the optimal
strategy might be.

00:13:41.391 --> 00:13:42.910
There's just lots of things
you don't know about

00:13:42.911 --> 00:13:43.840
the problem in practice.

00:13:43.841 --> 00:13:47.210
So in particular for
TB in India we found initial

00:13:47.211 --> 00:13:50.180
prevalence rates, so the number
of infected agents in each

00:13:50.181 --> 00:13:52.710
group are really uncertain
because a lot of patients don't

00:13:52.711 --> 00:13:54.970
report to approved
treatment facilities.

00:13:54.971 --> 00:13:55.750
And we also don't

00:13:55.751 --> 00:13:57.660
know these contact patterns
between the groups.

00:13:57.661 --> 00:13:59.530
So I don't really
know what is the rate

00:13:59.531 --> 00:14:00.970
at which people who
are 20 years old

00:14:00.971 --> 00:14:03.000
come into contact with people
who are 40 years old, right?

00:14:03.001 --> 00:14:05.870
These are just things that we
don't really have good data on.

00:14:05.871 --> 00:14:08.650
And so, I'm not going to go
into really any detail about

00:14:08.651 --> 00:14:09.860
the algorithm here.

00:14:09.861 --> 00:14:15.770
But the gist of it is that
we construction optimization

00:14:15.771 --> 00:14:18.490
problems, or modelling
these different tradeoffs.

00:14:18.491 --> 00:14:20.867
And so our approach to
the DOMO algorithm has sort of

00:14:20.868 --> 00:14:23.688
a principled way of deciding on
the optimal treatment in a way

00:14:23.689 --> 00:14:26.510
that accounts for the different
sorts of the demographic and

00:14:26.511 --> 00:14:29.389
epidemiological factors in the
problem and also accounts for

00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:32.486
the incompleteness of our data,
right, so it solves a stochastic

00:14:32.487 --> 00:14:34.845
problem where we have
only uncertain knowledge.

00:14:34.846 --> 00:14:38.087
So we only have sort of
distributions over what many of

00:14:38.088 --> 00:14:40.835
the parameters of
the problem might look at.

00:14:40.836 --> 00:14:43.286
And so
we conduct an evaluation and

00:14:43.287 --> 00:14:47.030
simulation of the policies
produced by DOMO.

00:14:47.031 --> 00:14:50.570
And so we use existing data from
a variety of sources right?

00:14:50.571 --> 00:14:54.230
So government reports,
the RNTC program,

00:14:54.231 --> 00:14:57.650
the epidemiological
literature and so on.

00:14:57.651 --> 00:15:00.630
And then given that there's
still uncertainty in the data

00:15:00.631 --> 00:15:03.210
then we construct appropriate
sort of distributions over

00:15:03.211 --> 00:15:04.509
the parameters that
we don't know about.

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:09.900
And here as a brief snap shot of
the results so we're comparing

00:15:09.901 --> 00:15:12.741
our algorithm to, so we compared
it to our range to different

00:15:12.742 --> 00:15:14.510
characteristics and strategies
that you could think of for

00:15:14.511 --> 00:15:17.040
the problems and
there's a few of them here.

00:15:17.041 --> 00:15:21.600
And so these strategies
are you know what if you just

00:15:21.601 --> 00:15:24.240
focus on the groups with
the highest prevalence rates.

00:15:24.241 --> 00:15:27.155
What if you extrapolate from
the status quo treatment rates,

00:15:27.156 --> 00:15:29.470
right, just focus your resources
in proportion to what's being

00:15:29.471 --> 00:15:30.390
done now.

00:15:30.391 --> 00:15:32.160
And lastly,
what quote high degree,

00:15:32.161 --> 00:15:34.810
so if you focus on the groups
that have the highest amount of

00:15:34.811 --> 00:15:35.990
contact with others.

00:15:35.991 --> 00:15:39.300
And we find that our algorithm
can substantially improve on

00:15:39.301 --> 00:15:44.110
these existing sorts of policies
and advert thousands of persons

00:15:44.111 --> 00:15:47.210
with tuberculosis each year
compared to these strategies.

00:15:49.700 --> 00:15:53.220
So this is where we're at right
now with a model and algorithm

00:15:53.221 --> 00:15:56.466
for targeting TB average
campaigns, we have promising

00:15:56.467 --> 00:15:59.503
results but this is very
much an outgoing project and

00:15:59.504 --> 00:16:02.904
I hope to talk to you many about
in this area during breaks.

00:16:02.905 --> 00:16:03.628
Thank you.

00:16:03.629 --> 00:16:10.428
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much, so if you

00:16:10.429 --> 00:16:13.400
have any clarification questions
while the next speaker sets up?

00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:17.406
Just ask a quick question.

00:16:17.407 --> 00:16:23.046
&gt;&gt; I have one, so one, at the
end of your talk you mentioned

00:16:23.047 --> 00:16:27.930
that the DST in the initial
stage is the best.

00:16:27.931 --> 00:16:29.856
Does it mean you have to do for
every patient or

00:16:29.857 --> 00:16:32.340
is there any filter at all you
can do to suspect that someone

00:16:32.341 --> 00:16:33.376
has drug resistant TB?

00:16:33.377 --> 00:16:37.180
&gt;&gt; So in the context of our
model we assumed that the only

00:16:37.181 --> 00:16:39.950
information that the physician
had was national prevelance

00:16:39.951 --> 00:16:42.370
levels, so 3% of the patients
would be drug resistant,

00:16:42.371 --> 00:16:44.600
and everybody else would
be drug sensitive.

00:16:44.601 --> 00:16:47.680
And so we assume that everybody
would be exposed to DST in

00:16:47.681 --> 00:16:50.190
that case and it was still
cost effective to do it.

00:16:50.191 --> 00:16:53.220
So our objective function
there was taking into

00:16:53.221 --> 00:16:55.760
account of both costs and
health outcomes.

00:16:55.761 --> 00:16:58.131
And if you looked for
a long enough time at Horizon,

00:16:58.132 --> 00:17:00.724
it was in fact cost saving
because of the saved costs from

00:17:00.725 --> 00:17:02.784
not having to treat
downstream infection.

